Z||000000||Philphine||03-13-2001||07:39 PM||||i have a 1/2 din eq as part of the system in my '87 coupe. i had read an article on rear fill a while back that said it is good to play the rear speakers in mono. for me i have no choice because i just use 1 speaker in the rear anyway. the article influenced this decision. in this article it said to get the mono signal you could just combine the left and right rear out puts with a pair of 2 to 1 rca jacks. if you had 2 speakers in the rear you then used a set of 1 to 2 rca jacks to power 2 channels. i kinda wondered if it would short out the signal and even asked a local tech about it. he didn't like the idea. since this was an article in car stero review i did it any way.<P>when i made this decision i had 3 places to decide to make the signal mono. coming out of the head unit, the eq, or my x over. i choose the eq 'cause it was the cheapest and easiest to replace.<P>it's actually been this way over a year with no problems till a few days ago. it's dead. fuse is still good and no smell of burnt components. i can't come up with a reason for it going out except for maybe how i had the rear outputs wired. i bypassed it, the radio feeds the xover strait but i have no rear fill for the moment (also less engine noise, pretty much none).<P>so if you're still with me the question is this. besides it being a pretty cheap eq (california profile) do you think the way it was hooked up may have burnt it out? i want to get another eq or processor of some kind and hook it up the same way, but not if i'm gonna blow it, especially since i'll want a better one. it seems like if that were the problem it wouldn't have lasted so long hooked up that way. also carstereo review is a pretty reputable mag. i can't see them giving out advice that would be quite that damaging.<P>side note: i may get a 1/2 din bbe instead of a full eq. got one in my truck and it really makes it sound good. but at $180 i definitly don't want to blow it.<P>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 03-13-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||03-13-2001||08:37 PM||||First, Connecting the left channel and the right channel together isn't too good to do. You can do it at the low level side but you don't want to do it to the high level side. This is probably what killed the EQ. Over time the output amps ran hot and they finally died.<BR>Now if you are using the rear fill as a center channel, You can buy a mono amp and Y the RCA inputs to it. If you want a true center channel, Audio Control makes an ESP-3. This unit is a surround sound processor and a center channel processor. (Real center channel is L - R, where "Y"ing the left and right will give you L + R) You would still need an amp but you could buy a 15W mono amp and use it just for the rear. Or buy a 4-channel amp that you can bridge Channels 3 & 4 into a mono channel and have channels 1 & 2 go to the front speakers. I would get the center channel processor to do it. It may cost more but it will sound better. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||03-13-2001||09:11 PM||||i have center channel output through my xover, but it is for the front center. i'm hoping for a solution that won't make me have to do so much system reconfiguring. i may have to try to contact car stereo review to see what they might have to say about it. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MontanaFiero||03-13-2001||09:21 PM||||I have almost always used one sub instead of two. I feel it is not necessary to have more than one sub unless you are doing it for show. You can buy "bridgeable" amplifiers at almost any car stereo shop, and some even have a "mono" switch on it. So my advice... go ahead and replace your eq with whatever brand you want, but go the extra mile and use a bridgeable amp. (Bridgeable means you can combine the right and left high power output to make a powerful mono amplifier. Instructions on doing this are very specific, though)<P>What the he!! is a bbe?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">   <BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||03-13-2001||10:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>i have center channel output through my xover, but it is for the front center. i'm hoping for a solution that won't make me have to do so much system reconfiguring. i may have to try to contact car stereo review to see what they might have to say about it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the EQ has a center out, Why don't you just put the front and rear speakers in series? This would cut down power slightly but your just using them for seperation and fill, not as main drivers. If you want to control the level to the rear speaker, Just hook the speakers in paralell and put a 50 ohm pot or an 8 ohm resistor in-line to it. The pot will give you variable control, the 8 ohm will knock it down by 3db (half).<P>Car Stereo Review is going to tell you to add a lot more equipment as I suggested in the previous post. They will suggest equipment from one of their sponsors.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||03-13-2001||10:25 PM||||i guess i could try using the center channel out to run the rear too, i just don't want to. <P>montana, this isn't the sub. it is a single 4" full range speaker that is my rear fill. it raelly dosen't need alot of power, plus as i said, i'm hoping for a sulution that dosen't involve a lot of system reconfiguration. <P>a bbe unit is more or less a really nice tone control. really not a lot more than an outboard treble and bass control. it's a piece of equipment made originally for professional sound but packaged and adapted to car stereo use. i have one in the system in my truck ahead of a eq. the eq is still flat. i've never bothered to try and tweek it. i just adjust the bbe as it suits me. i got a kind of lower line jvc head unit with just one set of rca outs. i originally got the bbe to give me front and rear outs and fading, but i've come to like it for the sound enhancement. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||03-13-2001||10:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>i guess i could try using the center channel out to run the rear too, i just don't want to. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well there isn't many other options other than hooking it up the way you had it and making sure you buy one with a good warranty.<P><BR>||||reg||1
